ent creators who otherwise  wouldn't have a platform to so lucrative  ly distribute their content it's been a  really great thing for most independent  video creators I've been asking for a  more clear understanding of how the D  monetization works as it pertains to my  content I'm not creating content that's  fringe or extreme or disturbing or gory  or whatever the way that YouTube is  suppressing many independent channels as  if they were doing that is antithetical  to what made YouTube cool and  interesting to begin with but ultimately  it is YouTube's right it's my right to  complain about it it's my right to leave  YouTube or start my own YouTube if  that's what I want to do maybe it is  actually the best business decision for  them maybe it's just more worth it to  YouTube to take a better safe than sorry  approach to the way that they're de  monetizing me and to just push the  corporate media channels and not worry  too much about the collateral damage to  programs like mine in the end I don't  know that I can do that much about it  this is why it's so important for the  audience's of independent political talk  shows like ours to realize that the  show's can only exist unfettered through  direct support from the audience  especially if the YouTube algorithm and  the D monetization stuff gets worse it's  not just a talking point it's not a way  to get people to sign up for david  pakman show membership it's just reality  if we don't have reliable YouTube  revenue and we don't have reliable  financial support from the audience  we may have to figure out something else  maybe I'll have to get a different job  if you're not on YouTube to watch makeup  tutorials or cat videos and what you  want to do is watch content like ours  that's trying to shake things up trying  to deal with serious issues in an adult  way and rubbing some people the wrong  way admittedly then may  we all need to assess our roles as  consumers of shows like this one even  though these shows can be accessed for  free there are cost to the creator's  employees salaries employee health  insurance rent internet electricity  equipment connectivity so many other  things a political commentary show  relying more on direct support from the  audience than on YouTube ad revenue is  actually probably a better paradigm for  everybody involved it means that if you  are a patron of something that you value  and you're educated and entertained by  it instead of you and your clicks  becoming a commodity to YouTube's bottom  line through your cable subscription or  watching ads or whatever else it would  mean content creators would be more free  to do and say what they want  unbeaten to advertisers and that freedom  and integrity is something that's  crucial for shows and put the political  commentary sphere it probably also means  that you might even end up with more  content creators to choose from because  they'd be better funded or you'd have  higher quality content because people  doing this part-time could focus on it  full-time as an example when you pay for  content you're not just getting what you  paid for you're investing in something  you value so that it can sustain and  grow and improve so this is not a  promotion for our patreon page or our  membership program this is something I  personally find it not only fascinating  but of crucial importance from a Media  Studies point of view and it's something  that applies to the entire digital media  industry these are my thoughts but it is  something that is affecting online  political shows like this one and I do  believe that independent political shows  are particularly important to protect  when we see this administration wanting  to come up with new and novel ways to  suppress speech they don't like and to  mandate speech they do like be published  regardless of what a private company  wants to do what we ultimately need is a  big picture meta shift in how we think  about online content how we consume it  and what we are willing to do to sustain  it and guard it from outside influences  and I hope that's what you'll take away  from this today
 

